5,0,5,5,0,5,5,5,5,0,5,5,0,5,5],[7,7,7,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,5,5,5,5,0,0,5,0,0],[5,5,0,5,5,0,5,0,5,0,5,0,5,5,0,5,0,5,5],[5,5,5,5,5,0,5,5,0,0,5,5,5,0,5,5,5,5,5],[5,5,5,5,0,0,5,0,0,0,0,0,0,0,0,5,0,5,5],[5,5,0,0,5,0,5,5,5,0,5,5,0,0,5,5,7,2,7],[5,5,5,5,7,2,7,5,5,5,0,5,0,0,5,0,2,4,7],[5,0,5,0,7,4,2,5,0,0,5,0,0,5,0,5,7,7,7],[5,5,5,5,7,2,7,5,5,0,5,5,5,5,0,5,5,5,5],[0,5,5,5,5,5,0,5,0,5,0,5,5,0,5,5,5,0,5],[5,5,0,5,0,0,0,0,5,0,5,5,5,0,0,0,0,5,5],[5,5,5,0,5,0,5,0,5,0,5,0,0,5,0,0,5,5,5]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The rule is \"every 3×3 window that holds a group of 2s gets a 4 in the centre and a 7 frame\". It needs one more condition to rule out a false window.\n\n## What the grid is made of\n\nThe grids are a noisy 0/5 texture with background 5. A few small groups of red cells (colour 2) are scattered through it. In every output, each red group becomes part of a complete **3×3 window**:\n- the window's centre gets colour **4**;\n- every other background cell in the window becomes **7**;\n- the original 2s stay where they are.\n\nSo each red group marks three corners or edge cells of an X-like 3×3 pattern. The output fills in the rest of that 3×3 box around a 4.\n\n## Locating each window\n\nFor a red group, the right window is the 3×3 box that:\n1. contains every 2 of that group;\n2. has a background (5) cell at its centre;\n3. contains no 0 cell at all, since the stamp never overwrites a 0.\n\nMy first inspect grouped the 2s only by touching (diagonal) adjacency. That split some groups that clearly belong together. My second inspect used a simpler test: the window contains some 2s, no 0, a 5 centre, and no other 2s in the 5×5 ring around it.\n\nThat test matched pairs 2 and 3 exactly. In pair 1 it produced one extra window, centred at (12,17). That window holds only the 2 at (11,18). That 2 really belongs with the 2s at (9,16),(9,17), whose proper window is centred at (10,17).\n\n**Fix:** group the 2s by Chebyshev distance ≤ 2 rather than strict adjacency. Then require the window to contain the *whole* group. The 2 at (11,18) is distance 2 from (9,17), so all three are one group. The (12,17) window misses two of them and is rejected, while (10,17) contains all three.\n\nI keep the 5×5-ring check as an extra safeguard. It did not reject any correct window in the train pairs.\n\n## Applying to the test\n\nThe test has four red groups:\n- {(3,13),(4,13),(4,14)}\n- {(6,1),(7,2)}\n- {(12,17),(13,16)}\n- {(13,5),(14,6),(15,5)}\n\nThey are far apart, and the unrefined check already found exactly one window per group, at centres (3,14), (7,1), (13,17) and (14,5). Each gets a 4 at its centre and 7s on its remaining 5-cells. I emit the canvas once after each window is stamped, then emit the final grid.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    bg = background(g)\n    out = g.copy()\n    reds = list(zip(*np.where(g == 2)))\n    # cluster 2s by Chebyshev distance <= 2\n    n = len(reds)\n    parent = list(range(n))\n    def find(i):\n        while parent[i] != i:\n            parent[i] = parent[parent[i]]\n            i = parent[i]\n        return i\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            if max(abs(reds[i][0] - reds[j][0]), abs(reds[i][1] - reds[j][1])) <= 2:\n                parent[find(i)] = find(j)\n    groups = {}\n    for i in range(n):\n        groups.setdefault(find(i), []).append(reds[i])\n    windows = []\n    for grp in groups.values():\n        for cy in range(1, H - 1):\n            for cx in range(1, W - 1):\n                if g[cy, cx] != bg:\n                    continue\n                w = g[cy - 1:cy + 2, cx - 1:cx + 2]\n                if (w == 0).any():\n                    continue\n                if not all(abs(y - cy) <= 1 and abs(x - cx) <= 1 for y, x in grp):\n                    continue\n                big = g[max(0, cy - 2):cy + 3, max(0, cx - 2):cx + 3]\n                if (big == 2).sum() != (w == 2).sum():\n                    continue\n                windows.append((cy, cx))\n    for cy, cx in windows:\n        for y in range(cy - 1, cy + 2):\n            for x in range(cx - 1, cx + 2):\n                if g[y, x] == bg:\n                    out[y, x] = 7\n        out[cy, cx] = 4\n        emit(out, f\"Completed the 3x3 window centred at ({cy},{cx}) around its red group: 4 at the centre, 7 on the remaining background cells.\")\n    emit(out, \"All red groups are completed into 3x3 windows.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"3. contains no 0 cell at all, since the stamp never overwrites a 0."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"I emit the canvas once after each window is stamped, then emit the final grid."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"4b21a7fd0291a37e85d515e5b8b9c75adfdf6dc32a028b9af382a0b3ddadbaba","task_sha256":"521910be04ed8795e1089b15680f92cc094f9cc6caef8840aabd82be850532c7","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
